I haven't played around with it yet, but I think the idea is awesome. 
What's needed is a demonstration of what the new level compiler can
do.  (Also, maybe a newsgroup article on what can be done with it, and
of course things like loop support.)  Maybe if someone were to make a
Nethack variant using the updated level compiler people would see the
potential in it.  On that note:

Nethack does a lot of things well, but one of the things that I,
personally, find a little lacking is the degree of variety in the
dungeon.

The majority of Nethack levels fall into the categories of Dungeon,
Cavern and Maze.  All deviation from that pattern comes in the form of
special levels.  While there are not a few of those, compared to the
size of the dungeon they're still rather uncommon.

In my mind, the two things that Nethack needs, gameplay-wise, at the
moment are: shorter/more interesting Gehennom and more level variety,
which plays into the Gehennom thing.

But what to add?  What kinds of places and situations would you like
to see in Nethack?  I'm not talking about special levels, like the Big
Room or the Valley of the Dead, nor am I talking about new things to
put in dungeons like tombstones and kitchen sinks.  I'm talking about
everyday dungeon environments, things that could appear on level one
or twenty.  I think the basic
roughly-two-rows-of-rooms-with-connecting-tunnels map dynamic could
use some adjustment.

I present here my own starter list of YANIs for new "ordinary" level
types.  I hope other posters will add to the list.  Anyway, here's
what I'd like so see, and god and source code willing, will try to
add:

- Underground River levels.  Simply, start with an ordinary dungeon
level.  Then, plot a river passing through it.  Any place that would
be a wall or rock that's overlaid by the river is turned into water. 
Any tunnel sections become ordinary floor, or alternatively ice or
open (and uncloseable) drawbridge.  Plain floor is left as-is.  In the
event that the river cuts through a shop, the shop gets to keep its
wall.

Game effects: greater visibility (the player can see up and down the
river), greater danger (be careful not to step off into the water, or
destroy ice or drawbridge sections).  If a level uses ice and/or
drawbridge to cross the water, if something happens to those spaces
then the player might need to acquire levitation or a wand of cold to
get across, so those variations shouldn't appear in the higher areas
of the dungeon.

Further mutations: In deeper levels (below 12), replace the water
sometimes with lava.  Also on some levels, make the "river" be an
earthquake fissure, which conceptionally would be a stretch of
unescapable trap doors.


Rooms in Mazes:
Mazes are more interesting if there's a reason to explore them.  If
the rooms have a standard shape the player will suspect there's a room
there when all the adjacent passages seem to veer away from that
unusual rectangular area.  We already have something slightly like
this with the minitowers deep in Gehennom.  Note that this sort of
implies that mazes should occaisionally higher in the dungeon (shops
in Gehennom?), which itself implies that mazes should be able to exist
without the requirement of minotaurs.

And if we have mazes higher up, then why not some with tree walls
("hedge mazes"), and water and lava trenches instead of "real" walls.


Hallways with sub-rooms:
In other words, "realistic" dungeons composed of one or more hallways,
possibly long, with various smaller rooms groups along its length with
doors.  Anything cool appearing in these levels would be placed within
the cells.  Large cells at corners would be where special rooms would
go.  Because of the cramped surroundings, boulders wouldn't appear
here.  Maybe put some ornamental topiary in vestibules, too.

Further mutations:
Peaceful prisoners in cells, flooded cells, tombstones, etc.  Anything
to give the level that cozy Tower of London feel.


Special levels: Flip horizontally or vertically
The redesigned level compiler patch supported this feature in earlier
versions.  I think it would be great for keeping players on their
toes.  The entire map for the special level, as well as all its
contents, would be mirrored horizontally or vertically if they had the
necessary flag.  Note that Gauntlet II did this to levels randomly.


Miscellaneous level ideas:
All of these modifiers would be applied to any of the above types, or
the standard random level types.  Also given are suggests dungeon
depths and odds that a given level would be of this type.
* Boobytrapped levels (level five and below, 1 in 20 levels): place a
large number of traps (30+) on this level, of all the available types.
 The idea here is that traps aren't deadly enough to kill unless the
player is already low on hitpoints.
* Low-magic levels (level eight and below, 1 in 20 levels): magic
costs as much to cast here as it does with carrying the Amulet. 
Monsters don't use magic while here.  There would probably need to be
a special message upon entering one of these levels.
* Foggy levels (level five and below, 1 in 20 levels): frequent clouds
block line-of-sight within rooms.

